to-yanyi-liu-source : List Word
to-yanyi-liu-source = lines-of (cons "# Yanyi Liu"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Cornell Tech, advised by Rafael Pass and Elaine Shi. Your research builds cryptography on standard complexity-theoretic assumptions, with the worst-case hardness of NP as the goal. Everything here that rests on your results credits them to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work established"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Cryptography has usually been built one assumption at a time: factoring for one primitive, a lattice problem for another, a random oracle where nothing else fits. The field treats “one-way functions exist” as the floor everything stands on, an assumption taken rather than understood."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your 2020 paper with Pass replaced that floor with a question about description. One-way functions exist if and only if time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity K^t is mildly hard on average. Whether a secret can exist is the same question as whether, under a time bound, one can tell how short a description a string has. The existence of cryptography becomes a fact about compression."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The work since has pushed that equivalence toward the worst case. *On the Possibility of Basing Cryptography on EXP ≠ BPP* won Best Paper at CRYPTO 2021. *Hardness Along the Boundary* (CRYPTO 2025): one-way functions exist if and only if a boundary version of K^t-randomness is not in ioBPP, unconditionally for the probabilistic pK^t and under strengthened derandomization for plain K^t. The ITCS 2026 paper: boundary hardness of the standard randomized K^t is enough; under E ⊄ ioSIZE[2^{o(n)}] the characterization holds for plain K^t, by Goldberg–Sipser language compression; and it gives the first worst-case to average-case reduction for exact K^poly, improving on Hirahara's gap version."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field reads this as meta-complexity. It is more: the boundary between what can be hidden and what cannot is the boundary between data that compresses and data that does not, under a clock. That decides which systems of shared knowledge can be trusted."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Where it lands in hyperactivity"
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Content addressing rests on a stated hardness premise.** Every definition is stored under its content address with its dependency references"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8).")
  (cons "The paper states what that address is worth: it identifies a representation under the store's collision-resistance or injectivity assumptions, and does not prove semantic equivalence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8).")
  (cons "The store's identity of content is conditional on a cryptographic premise named in the text. Collision-resistant hashing implies one-way functions, and your theorem says what one-way functions are. So the store's identity rests, in the end, on the hardness you characterized."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Hardness assumptions are premises the language keeps.** As Avik put it (2026-11-18): “the interaction system can model crypto primitives & hardness assumptions as well.” In the paper a requirement is a chosen dependent family and the reasoning that establishes it a term"
  (cons (cite s1-p24 "(§1);")
  (cons "premises it does not prove stay explicit, as the authenticity of external observations and machine semantics do"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8).")
  (cons "A codebase holds programs together with their proofs, equivalences and dependency certificates"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8),")
  (cons "so a protocol in the graph does not say “secure under standard assumptions”: it depends on the hypothesis it consumes, by reference."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Your equivalences become paths.** An equivalence of types gives a path between them"
  (cons (cite s4-p4 "(4.1),")
  (cons "and transport along it carries a dependent predicate, or an operation with its proof, to the other side"
  (cons (cite s4-p14 "(§4).")
  (cons "Propositional truncation keeps the existence of an equivalence but not a particular one to compute with"
  (cons (cite s2-p8 "(§2),")
  (cons "so where a construction needs a particular function rather than the bare existence claim, it needs your reduction itself, which is what your proofs supply. As your results tighten toward the worst case, each step is one more such relation, and every dependent result records which version it rests on."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The clock has its own place.** An equality proof does not assert that two executions take equal time; such quantities are additional observations"
  (cons (cite s3-2-p7 "(§3.2),")
  (cons "and a performance claim is a separate typed statement about the same artifact"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p17 "(§10.6).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this serves your program"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your stated aim is cryptography from the worst-case hardness of NP. The remaining distance is a chain of conditional equivalences, each with its own derandomization or boundary hypothesis. Kept as papers, those hypotheses spread across footnotes. Kept in a store whose codebase holds programs with their proofs and dependencies, the chain is a structure: each link a relation with its evidence, each hypothesis a named premise, and what is still assumed read off the dependencies."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The equivalence of one-way functions with the hardness of time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity, the boundary formulation and its unconditional pK^t form, the use of language compression to reach plain K^t, and the worst-case to average-case reduction for exact K^poly are your work, with Rafael Pass. Wherever the graph uses them, it names you as their source. Avik would like you to read the work looking for what it makes of yours, and to tell him where it should go further."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Simons Institute: Yanyi Liu, https://simons.berkeley.edu/people/yanyi-liu"
  (cons "- Liu, Pass: On One-way Functions and Kolmogorov Complexity, https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.11514"
  (cons "- Cornell Tech: Best Paper at CRYPTO 2021, https://tech.cornell.edu/news/rafael-pass-and-yanyi-liu-win-best-paper-award-at-crypto-21"
  (cons "- Liu, Pass: Hardness Along the Boundary, https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1497"
  (cons "- Liu, Pass: One-way Functions and Boundary Hardness of Randomized Time-Bounded Kolmogorov Complexity, ITCS 2026, https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/2184"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
